  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tomorrow: Forecast remains on track for of elevated \nto critical fire weather conditions for the Inland northwest \ntoday. This mornings wave that brought virga showers and \nisolated lightning to southeastern BC has moved rapidly \nnortheast towards central Alberta, bringing in a drier airmass \nbehind it thanks to downsloping winds off the Cascades and \nsynoptic descent. Winds through much of the troposphere increase\nthis afternoon and evening as a trough offshore Vancouver \nIsland becomes neutral to slightly positive tilted tightening \nthe thermal gradient over WA. Red flag warnings are in effect \nuntil this evening in portions of central WA but the area of \nmost concern remains the Okanogan valley where deeper mixing and\nslope alignment will mix down gusts to around 25-35 mph with \nease. Pressure gradients become more aligned with the Cascade \ngaps this evening which will lead to a westerly wind shift \naround sunset on the Waterville Plateau and western Columbia \nBasin. The Inland northwest will be brushed by a dry cold front \ninto tomorrow morning bringing an even drier airmass to central \nWA. However, the further south and east you are tomorrow in the \nInland Northwest, there is a higher chance of seeing cirrus as \nPWATs are actually increasing thanks to moisture transport from \nthe monsoon high in the intermountain west. The cold front \npassage and cirrus will moderate temperatures into the 70s and \n80s tomorrow, slightly below average. Still am expecting \nelevated to locally critical fire weather conditions for the \nOkanogan valley Sunday as conditions look even drier but with \nwinds being weaker in the boundary layer, opted out of issuing \nanother red flag warning. \n\nMonday and Tuesday: Temperatures rebound back into the 80s and \n90s Monday and Tuesday as the upper low currently west of \nVancouver Island departs and subtle height rises take place. The\nbest chance for mid to high 90s will be further north and west \ntowards central WA as mid and high level clouds will knock down \ntemperatures in eastern WA and north ID a few degrees. Locations\nalong US-97 have a 20-50% chance of seeing 100+ degrees \nTuesday. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: Forecast confidence drops off quickly\nmid next week as models are struggling with a shortwave trough \noff the WA/OR coast. The gridded forecast fails to tell the \nstory. With a shortwave passage disrupting the monsoonal ridge, \nthat would open the door for increased chances for showers and \nthunderstorms, which would bring cooler temperatures. For \nexample, the LREF has a 10% chance of temperatures cooler than \n75 degrees at Omak Thursday. On the flip side, there is a 13% \nchance of warmer than 95 degrees. Wednesday and Thursday will \nneed to be closely watched for the potential of widespread \nlightning (confidence is still very low at this point). \nInterestingly enough forecast confidence increases for warm and \ndry conditions to return as ensembles show the monsoon high once\nagain impinging on the Inland Northwest beyond Thursday. /DB",
